<Layout id="preciseexceptions" title="Precise Exceptions">

  <h1>Precise Exceptions</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    The CMS translation of x86 into native translated blocks makes several assumptions and risks.
    This leads to a wide range of exceptions that needs to be dealt with.
  </p>


  <h2>Risks of Translation</h2>

  <p>
    CMS can make assumptions about the code it translates and execute operations
    in a different order from the original x86 program. These assumptions can
    sometimes fail or produce exceptions during native execution.
  </p>

  <p>
    Crusoe uses shadow registers + gated buffers to commit and rollback
    so that the processor can return to a consistent x86 state when this happens.
  </p>


  <h2>Shadow Registers</h2>

  <p>
    Crusoe keeps working registers for the current native
    execution state and shadow registers for the most recently
    committed x86 state.
  </p>

  <p>
    After a translation completes successfully, CMS executes a
    commit. This copies the working registers into
    their corresponding shadow registers, creating a new checkpoint.
  </p>


  <h2>Gated Store Buffer</h2>

  <p>
    Stores from a translation are held in a gated store buffer
    instead of being released immediately to the memory system.
  </p>

  <p>
    When the translation commits, the stores are released from the buffer into
    the normal memory hierarchy. If the translation is rolled back, stores that
    have not yet been committed can be discarded.
  </p>


  <h2>Common ways rollsbacks happen</h2>

  <h3>1. Page Fault</h3>

  <p>
    The most common is a page fault. Crusoe can roll back to the previous committed state so that CMS
    can handle the fault according to the original x86 semantics.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Translation Assumption Fails</h3>

  <p>
    CMS can make speculative assumptions when creating native code. If one
    fails, Crusoe rolls back to the last committed state. CMS then usually
    interprets the corresponding x86 instructions in their original program
    order.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the interpreter encounters the fault again, it can be deduced that this exception would occur in x86 program order too so CMS invokes the x86 exception
    handling procedure. If the interpreter does not encounter the fault, CMS
    treats it as a speculative translation failure rather than an actual x86
    exception.
  </p>

</Layout>